# Source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2" (solved), 3 c). The invariant proposed by the solved sheet is the
# candidate of the second choice; it fails initialisation, so the exercise repairs it.
id: integer-square-root
title: Integer square root: repairing a candidate invariant
topic: Loop invariants
level: 3
source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 3 c
tags: invariant, failed candidate, refine, disjunction

task:
  The loop finds the smallest $i$ whose square is larger than $n$. A
  first candidate for the loop invariant is
  $s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$. Check it, repair it if
  a check fails, and prove the triple with the repaired invariant. Choose
  the invariant above the steps: the proof succeeds with the repaired
  invariant, the first one, and fails with the first candidate, the
  second one.

given:
  Loop condition B: $s <= n$

pre: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0
post: i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n < i * i

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (s <= n) do {
    {I && s <= n}
    {R2}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    s := i * i       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(s <= n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

choice: Repaired invariant, with i = 0 ∨ (i − 1)² ≤ n
outcome: proves

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The first candidate $s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$
  fails initialisation: at the start $i = 0$, so $(i - 1) * (i - 1) = 1$,
  and with $n = 0$ the conjunct $1 <= 0$ is false. The second choice
  above the steps shows this check.

  The conjunct $(i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$ says that the previous value of
  $i$ was small enough. Before the first iteration there is no previous
  value, so we require it only when $i != 0$. We also keep $0 <= n$ from
  $P$: the loop does not change $n$, and the exit needs this fact.
set: I = s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $s <= n$: initialisation, preservation and
  conclusion.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0 ==> s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)
why:
  With $s = 0$ and $i = 0$: $0 = 0 * 0$ and $0 <= 0$ hold, $0 <= n$ is in
  $P$, and the disjunction holds by its first part $i = 0$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of s := i * i for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «s <= n : B»} i := i + 1; s := i * i {«I»}
  We compute the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $s$ with $i * i$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := i * i, I)
  = (s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n))[s <- i * i]
  = i * i = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)     // every s is replaced by i * i
  = 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)     // i * i = i * i is true, and true && A is A
set: R1 = 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $R1$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = (0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n))[i <- i + 1]
  = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i + 1 = 0 || (i + 1 - 1) * (i + 1 - 1) <= n)     // every i is replaced by i + 1
  = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i + 1 = 0 || i * i <= n)     // i + 1 - 1 is i
  = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i = -1 || i * i <= n)        // subtract 1 from both sides of i + 1 = 0
set: R2 = 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i = -1 || i * i <= n)

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && s <= n, R2
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. By the consequence
  rule, preservation holds when $I$ and $B$ together imply $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «s <= n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n) && s <= n ==> 0 <= i + 1 && 0 <= n && (i = -1 || i * i <= n)
why:
  $0 <= i$ gives $0 <= i + 1$, and $0 <= n$ is in $I$. The second
  disjunct $i * i <= n$ holds: $i * i = s$ by $I$, and $s <= n$ by $B$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(s <= n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and the loop condition $B$ is false. This
  must imply the postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(s <= n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: s = i * i && 0 <= i && 0 <= n && (i = 0 || (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n) && !(s <= n) ==> i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n < i * i
why:
  $i >= 0$ is in $I$. $!(s <= n)$ is $n < s$, and $s = i * i$, so
  $n < i * i$.

  For $(i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$ we look at the disjunction of $I$. If
  $i = 0$, then $s = 0$, and $n < 0$ contradicts $0 <= n$. So $i != 0$,
  and the second disjunct $(i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$ holds.
result: The triple is valid.

choice: First candidate, with (i − 1)² ≤ n
outcome: fails

step: The candidate invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We check the first candidate. It says that $s$ is the square of $i$,
  and that the previous value $i - 1$ has a square not larger than $n$.
set: I = s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n

step: P does not imply I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  No statement comes before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply
  $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: n >= 0 && s = 0 && i = 0 ==> s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n
status: fails
counterexample: n = 0, s = 0, i = 0
why:
  At the start $i = 0$, so $(i - 1) * (i - 1) = 1$. With $n = 0$ the
  conjunct $1 <= 0$ is false. The candidate rejects a start state, so it
  is too strong.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of s := i * i for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  >>> {«I» && «s <= n : B»} i := i + 1; s := i * i {«I»}
  We substitute $s$ with $i * i$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := i * i, I)
  = (s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)[s <- i * i]
  = i * i = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n     // every s is replaced by i * i
  = i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n                      // i * i = i * i is true, and true && A is A
set: R1 = i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n

step: R2, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $R1$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = (i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n)[i <- i + 1]
  = i + 1 >= 0 && (i + 1 - 1) * (i + 1 - 1) <= n     // every i is replaced by i + 1
  = i + 1 >= 0 && i * i <= n                         // i + 1 - 1 is i
set: R2 = i + 1 >= 0 && i * i <= n

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && s <= n, R2
text:
  >>> «I» && «s <= n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n && s <= n ==> i + 1 >= 0 && i * i <= n
why:
  $i >= 0$ gives $i + 1 >= 0$. $i * i = s$ by $I$, and $s <= n$ by $B$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(s <= n), Q
text:
  >>> «I» && «!(s <= n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: s = i * i && i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n && !(s <= n) ==> i >= 0 && (i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n < i * i
why:
  $i >= 0$ and $(i - 1) * (i - 1) <= n$ are in $I$, and $!(s <= n)$ with
  $s = i * i$ gives $n < i * i$. Preservation and conclusion hold, but
  initialisation fails, so this candidate cannot be used. The repaired
  invariant, the first choice, weakens it on the start state.

summary:
  The failed initialisation check gave the start state $n = 0$, $i = 0$,
  $s = 0$, which the first candidate rejected. The repair weakens the
  candidate on that state with the disjunct $i = 0$. The conjunct
  $0 <= n$ is needed at the exit to rule out $i = 0$.
